1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
A novelist’s nightmare. One change, and the story stops making sense.

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:09,300
Mara dies at the end of chapter two.

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,660
So why is she still speaking in chapter three?

4
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:16,460
This is RETCON—backfill for stories.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:21,060
It’s an Apache Airflow plugin, with the writer’s workspace built right

6
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:21,720
into Airflow.

7
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,140
Here’s The Aster Protocol, a five-chapter mystery.

8
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:31,060
I’m changing Mara’s fate: she dies at the end of chapter two.

9
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:34,060
First, let’s see what that would break.

10
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:40,340
Three later chapters mention her. Two still give her dialogue after she’s

11
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:46,300
dead. That’s a problem. Chapter five only remembers her, so that passage

12
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:46,880
is fine.

13
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:53,380
Now I apply the change. A character-canon Asset event triggers the repair

14
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:56,520
workflow. I’ve shortened the model wait here.

15
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:02,400
Airflow uses dynamic task mapping to send only the broken paragraphs to

16
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:08,000
its Common AI provider. The model proposes replacements; RETCON checks

17
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:12,800
them. In this run, one needed a corrective attempt before it passed.

18
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,340
Then Airflow stops at its native human approval task.

19
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:22,000
The original manuscript stays intact while the writer reviews the

20
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:26,900
changes. Passing a check doesn’t make something good writing—the author

21
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:27,760
still decides.

22
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:34,520
Here are the versions side by side. Ivo takes over Mara’s dialogue, while

23
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:36,660
the surrounding paragraphs stay as they were.

24
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:40,180
I’m happy with these changes, so I’ll approve them.

25
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:42,260
I could also keep the original.

26
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:45,380
Approval resumes the Airflow workflow.

27
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:50,500
RETCON publishes a new manuscript version, and Airflow records the

28
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,460
published-manuscript Asset event. Three chapters checked, two paragraphs

29
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:57,780
repaired, three chapters untouched.

30
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:03,500
And here’s chapter five. Sera still remembers Mara’s laugh.

31
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:09,560
That memory belongs in the story. I can now export the revised manuscript

32
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,380
as Markdown and keep writing.

33
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:18,020
That’s RETCON. One plugin, from a story change to an approved revision.

34
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:21,840
Change the past. Keep your story whole.
